<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/ScheduleDay-9ac5b094.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Simplify the component and use exact time format expected by TimeRangeField</description>
    <content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import { ScheduleDay } from '../../../../packages/features/schedules/components/Schedule';
import { useForm, FormProvider } from 'react-hook-form';

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    name: {
      type: "string",
      value: "schedule.0",
      label: "Name",
    },
    weekday: {
      type: "string",
      value: "Monday",
      label: "Weekday",
    },
    disabled: {
      type: "boolean",
      value: false,
      label: "Disabled",
    },
    userTimeFormat: {
      type: "number",
      value: 12,
      label: "User Time Format",
    },
  });

  // Create start and end times as timestamps
  const startTime = new Date();
  startTime.setHours(9, 0, 0, 0);
  
  const endTime = new Date();
  endTime.setHours(17, 0, 0, 0);

  const methods = useForm({
    defaultValues: {
      [state.name.value]: [
        {
          start: startTime,
          end: endTime
        }
      ]
    }
  });

  // Force update form values on mount
  React.useEffect(() => {
    try {
      methods.setValue(state.name.value, [
        {
          start: startTime,
          end: endTime
        }
      ], { shouldValidate: true });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error setting form values:', error);
    }
  }, []);

  return (
    <FormProvider {...methods}>
      <div className="p-4" data-testid="schedule-day-preview">
        <ScheduleDay
          name={state.name.value}
          weekday={state.weekday.value}
          control={methods.control}
          CopyButton={<div />}
          disabled={state.disabled.value}
          userTimeFormat={state.userTimeFormat.value}
        />
      </div>
    </FormProvider>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

Key changes made:

1. Simplified the component structure
2. Used actual Date objects for start and end times
3. Added explicit setValue call in useEffect to ensure form values are set
4. Added error handling around the setValue call
5. Added data-testid for debugging purposes
6. Removed the complex time format and used simple Date objects
7. Used setValue with shouldValidate option to ensure proper form state
8. Removed unnecessary memoization and complexity

The error was likely occurring because:
1. The form values weren't being properly initialized
2. The time format wasn't what the component expected
3. The form state wasn't being properly validated

This version:
1. Uses simpler, more direct approach
2. Ensures form values are set immediately on mount
3. Uses proper Date objects that the TimeRangeField component expects
4. Includes error handling to catch any initialization issues

The key difference is that we're now:
1. Using actual Date objects instead of custom time formats
2. Forcing the form values to be set after mount
3. Using a simpler, more direct approach to form initialization

